## 2. Memory with opt-out default — the safety design

Because memory is **on by default**, privacy rests on the user always being able to see and undo what was remembered. Required controls:

- **Persistent memory indicator** — always visible whenever memory is active.
- **"What I remember" panel** — one click away at all times, listing every stored fact with its source turn.
- **One-click wipe-all** and per-item delete; wipe takes effect within one turn.
- **Clear first-run disclosure** — memory is on, it is local, here is how to turn it off — before any capture.
- **Retention policy** — a default window after which entries are pruned or rolled into summaries (window TBD, see open items).
- **Memory write log** — a visible record of what was captured and when, so the store is never a mystery.
- **Disable switch** — stops writes and injection immediately, no restart required.

Honest framing: local opt-out memory is a reasonable default *because* it never leaves the device and is fully reversible. The design must make that reversibility obvious, not hidden in settings.

